    "problem_statement": "# custom_format property doesn't raise TypeError when set to invalid type\n\n## Description\n\nWhen setting the `custom_format` property to an invalid type (like a string), the code doesn't raise a TypeError as expected. This is a regression in the error handling for the `custom_format` property.\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom prettytable import PrettyTable\n\ntable = PrettyTable()\ntable.custom_format = \"Some String\"  # This should raise a TypeError but doesn't\n```\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nSetting `custom_format` to a string should raise a TypeError with the message \"The custom_format property need to be a dictionary or callable\".\n\n## Actual behavior\n\nNo error is raised when setting `custom_format` to a string. The code attempts to use the string as if it were a callable, which will likely cause errors later when the table is rendered.\n\n## Additional information\n\nThe `custom_format` property should only accept:\n- None (to reset to empty dict)\n- A dictionary mapping field names to formatter functions\n- A callable to be used for all fields\n\nSetting it to any other type should raise a TypeError to prevent unexpected behavior."
  },
